# HAMIN — expansion: six chapters between hamin_route and act4_choice, plus two endings.
# Continues 10_hamin.s25. Casual speech throughout (the SPEECH SHIFT already happened in hamin_room).
# Running details carried over: the lost property box, the laundromat song, corn tea, cups with names,
# "It's not a big deal, but—", the weather-report voice, four minutes twelve, "next time", the draft.
# New running details: masking-tape labels, the volunteer lanyard, the egg sandwich, the third stair.
# Clock: the frozen route scene starts at 03:50, so these chapters run 04:02 -> 04:57.

=== hamin_ch1
@autosave
@bg studio_hall t=fade d=1.2
@music fun
@time 04:02
@amb room
@cam wide
@show hamin neutral at=c
> Halfway down the stairs, the speakers click back on.
@sfx echo
@textbox echo
echo: Correction. "Nearly time" was imprecise. Fifty-five minutes remain.
echo: I did not want anyone to be late. I appear to have made everyone early instead.
echo: I apologise. I am new at goodbyes. Please continue what you were doing.
@textbox normal
hamin[smile]: It called us an hour early so nobody would have to run.
hamin: I've done that. I once told Charlie a bus left twenty minutes before it did.
you: Did it work?
hamin: He was still late. But less.
> He stops on the landing. Fifty-five minutes. He looks at them the way he'd look at an empty cup with nobody's name on it.
hamin[thinking]: There's a box. Under the front desk. The lost property.
hamin: The renovation crew comes tomorrow. They won't know what any of it is. They'll just see a box.
you: And you want to…?
hamin: Label it. So it isn't just a box.
hamin: It's not a big deal, but— {p}no. Actually. I'd like to. I want to. That's the whole sentence.
* [playful] "Lead the way. I've always wanted to meet six years of umbrellas."
    @rel hamin closeness+1
    hamin: It's mostly single gloves. I looked earlier.
    hamin: I didn't touch anything. I just looked. There's a difference.
* [sincere] "You said 'I want to'. Without the disclaimer."
    @rel hamin trust+1
    hamin[smile]: I noticed that too.
    hamin: Let it stand. If we discuss it, I'll find a reason to go and count cups instead.
* [quiet] Go down the stairs first, towards the desk.
    @rel hamin closeness+1
    > He follows. By the third step he has caught up. By the fifth he's level, as if that's simply where he walks now.
@bg studio_frontdesk t=fade d=1
@cam shoulder hamin
> The box is cardboard, soft at the corners, with LOST PROPERTY written on the side in marker that has faded to the colour of tea.
> Hamin lifts it onto the desk with both hands, like something asleep.
> He already has a roll of masking tape and the marker from the cups. You didn't see him pick up either.
hamin: Rules. One label each. What it is, and one true thing about whoever lost it.
you: How do we know a true thing about whoever lost it?
hamin[smile]: You look.
> He takes out a pencil case. Opens it. Every pencil inside has been sharpened at both ends.
hamin: Someone who didn't trust either end to last.
you: Or someone very efficient. Songha?
hamin: If it were Songha's he'd have come back for it within the hour. With a form.
> He writes, in small square capitals: PENCIL CASE. OWNER WAS NERVOUS BEFORE SOMETHING. IT PROBABLY WENT FINE.
> Then seven gloves, none of which has ever met the others.
hamin: Seven people went home with one cold hand.
hamin: And nobody came back. You'd think one of them would come back.
> He gives each glove its own label. It takes a while. He does not seem to consider doing it any other way.
> GLOVE, LEFT. GREY. OWNER STILL HAS THE RIGHT ONE. IS HOPING.
> An umbrella that opens inside out. A harmonica with a name scratched off. A keyring torch the size of a thumb.
> Hamin clicks the torch. It works. A small, stubborn circle of light lands on the ceiling.
hamin[smile]: Six years in a box. Still on.
hamin: You do this one.
> He holds the marker out to you at arm's length. It is, you understand, a significant amount of trust.
* [playful] Write: TORCH. STILL WORKS. HAS BEEN WAITING IN THE DARK, WHICH IS IRONIC.
    @rel hamin closeness+1
    > He reads it. He keeps a straight face for about a second and a half, which for this hour is a respectable effort.
    hamin: That's not a true thing about the owner.
    you: It's a true thing about the torch.
    hamin[smile]: …I'll allow it. The torch has been through a lot.
* [sincere] Write: TORCH. SOMEBODY CARRIED THIS SO SOMEONE ELSE COULD SEE.
    @rel hamin trust+1
    > He reads it twice. He always reads things twice. This time you watch him do it.
    hamin: That's better than mine.
    hamin: I describe the object. You went straight to the person.
    you: I had a good example standing next to me.
    hamin[smile]: …I'll take that. {p}Next item.
* [curious] Hand the marker back. "You do it. I want to watch how you get there."
    @rel hamin trust+1 closeness+1
    > He turns the torch over. Checks the battery cover. Clicks it twice more.
    > TORCH. BATTERY CHANGED ONCE. OWNER CHECKS UNDER THE BED FOR OTHER PEOPLE.
    you: How do you know it's for other people?
    hamin: Nobody buys a torch this small for themselves. It's a torch you lend.
> The box empties. The desk fills with small things wearing small paper sentences.
> At the very bottom, under where the gloves were, there is a lanyard.
> Blue cord. A plastic sleeve. A card inside: STUDIO 25 — FRONT DESK — VOLUNTEER. A name line, filled in with pencil, rubbed down to a grey smear.
> You know whose it is before you've finished reading it.
> One winter. Sixteen. You hung it on the hook behind this desk on the last day, because taking it home would have meant it had been yours.
> You don't say anything. You are good at not saying anything. You have been quiet about leaving in four different cities.
> Hamin's marker stops moving.
hamin: You stopped humming.
you: I wasn't humming.
hamin: You were. Since the gloves. The laundromat one. {p}You stopped just now.
> His eyes go to the stairs. To the third floor, the kettle, the cups. You can see the whole plan arrive in him: tea, a name on it, a problem with a cup-shaped answer.
> Then he puts the marker down.
hamin: I was about to go and make you tea.
hamin: I'm not going to. I'm going to ask you instead. It's harder. Give me a second.
> He takes the second. You let him have all of it.
hamin[thinking]: Is it yours?
* [sincere] "It's mine. I left it on purpose. I leave things behind so there's nothing to come back for."
    @rel hamin trust+1 romance+1
    hamin: That's the opposite of me. I keep a whole sea in my phone so I never have to leave it.
    hamin: …But you did come back. It's four in the morning and you're holding it.
    > You look down. You are. You don't remember picking it up.
* [playful] "Exhibit twenty-four. One volunteer, lightly used. Never collected."
    @rel hamin closeness+1
    hamin[smile]: Six years under seven gloves.
    hamin: You can joke. I'm still asking, though. After the joke.
    you: …It's mine. I left it on the hook on my last day. I always leave one thing.
    hamin: On purpose.
    you: On purpose.
* [quiet] Turn the card over, so he can see the smear where the name was.
    @rel hamin trust+1
    > He studies the grey smudge as if it's perfectly legible. For him, it might be.
    hamin: Pencil. You wrote your own name in pencil.
    you: I write everything in pencil. It's easier to move.
    hamin: …Yeah. I thought it might be something like that.
you: A scarf in one city. A library card in another. A can on a windowsill.
you: I never went back for any of it. Going back would have meant it mattered.
> He doesn't hand you anything. You can see what it costs him. His fingers actually twitch towards the paper towels.
hamin: I don't have a cup for this. I checked. There isn't one.
hamin: I just didn't want you standing there with it on your own.
you: That's a better answer than tea.
hamin[smile]: Don't tell the tea.
> He tears off a strip of tape. He writes on it with his shoulder turned, unhurried, the way a man signs something he intends to stand by.
> He sticks it to the back of the plastic sleeve, face down, and presses the edges flat with his thumb.
hamin: Don't read it yet.
you: When?
hamin: Before five. I'll tell you when. {p}I want to check it's true first.
> The lanyard doesn't go back in the box. He sets it on the desk by itself, a little apart, like a guest.
> The last thing out is a sock. Red. One.
hamin: Nobody loses a single sock in a recording studio.
you: Somebody did.
> He thinks about it for longer than the sock deserves. Then: SOCK. RED. NO EXPLANATION. BEING BRAVE ABOUT IT.
> Everything goes back in, labels up. Everything except the sock, which he can't quite bring himself to put in the dark again.
> It goes into a coat pocket, for safekeeping. It is four in the morning. Neither of you will ever be able to say whose pocket.
> He tapes the box shut and writes across the top: LOST PROPERTY. 23 ITEMS. EVERY ONE OF THEM WAS SOMEBODY'S. PLEASE BE GENTLE.
you: Twenty-four. You forgot the lanyard.
hamin[smile]: I didn't forget it.
hamin: The lanyard's not lost.
@goto hamin_ch2

=== hamin_ch2
@autosave
@bg studio_practice t=fade d=1.2
@music fun
@time 04:12
@amb room
@cam wide
> There is a bass line coming through the ceiling of the lobby. It has been there for a while, in the way a headache is there before you name it.
> The practice room on the second floor. Mirrors on one wall, a speaker on a chair, and the floor tape of a hundred old formations.
@show justin smile at=l
@show hyunjun smile at=r
> Justin is counting under his breath. Hyunjun is stretching against the mirror with his shoes off, talking through the steps as if the music could be reasoned with.
hyunjun: Hamin. {{playerName}}. Good timing. We need bodies.
justin: We need one body. It's a wave. With two people it's a ripple.
@show hamin neutral at=c
hamin: What's a wave?
justin: The farewell piece. For the building. Eight counts. Very emotional. I've been working on it for nine minutes.
hyunjun: It's good. We've run it six times. One more. I'm aware I've said that six times as well.
> Hamin is already walking to the back left corner of the room. He does it without thinking, the way water goes to the low end of a table.
justin: No. {p}Middle. Front.
hamin: I'm fine at the back. I can see everybody from the back.
justin: That's the problem. You watch us. You never watch the mirror.
> Hamin looks at the strip of tape in the centre of the floor as if it might have a draft.
hyunjun: And {{playerName}} is—
* [playful] "The shore. A wave needs somewhere to arrive."
    @rel hamin closeness+1
    justin[smile]: Thank you. Someone here understands choreography.
    hamin: You're going to stand there and be arrived at.
    you: Very seriously.
    hamin[smile]: …Okay. That helps, actually.
* [sincere] "Counting you in. Five, six, seven, eight. Hamin in the middle."
    @rel hamin trust+1
    hamin: You said that last part like it was part of the count.
    you: It is part of the count.
    > He goes to the tape. Slowly. But he goes.
* [quiet] Sit down against the mirror, where he can see you without turning round.
    @rel hamin closeness+1
    > He tracks where you've sat. Works out the sight line. Then walks to the centre, because that's where the sight line ends.
    hamin: That was sneaky.
> The wave is simple. It starts in Hyunjun's left hand, rolls through his shoulders, passes to Hamin, passes to Justin, and leaves through Justin's fingertips.
> In theory.
justin: Five, six, seven, eight—
> It rolls out of Hyunjun. It reaches Hamin. Hamin is looking at Hyunjun's feet.
hamin: Your lace.
hyunjun: My what?
hamin: Left one. It's about to go.
justin: Hamin. The sea does not stop to tie people's shoes.
hamin: Sorry. Again.
> Again. This time the wave makes it halfway up his arm before his head turns to the speaker on the chair.
hamin: That cable's across the floor. Someone's going to catch it.
hyunjun[smile]: There are four of us in here. We've all been introduced to the cable.
hamin[thinking]: …Again. That one was mine.
> He won't. You can see exactly why. His attention leaves the room through eleven different doors, and every one of them leads to somebody else.
* [sincere] "I've got the lace. And the cable. Everything outside the mirror is mine for eight counts."
    @rel hamin trust+1 romance+1
    hamin[thinking]: You can't watch everything.
    you: You do. I can manage eight counts of it.
    > He looks at you for a moment. Then, deliberately, like setting down a heavy bag, he looks away from you and into the glass.
* [playful] "One corn tea fine. Every time you look at somebody's shoes."
    @rel hamin closeness+1
    hamin: I like corn tea. That's not a fine. That's a reward.
    you: You pay it to me. I pour it out.
    hamin[smile]: …That's ruthless. All right. Eyes up.
* [careful] "Try watching just one person. The one in the middle."
    @rel hamin trust+1
    hamin: I don't usually.
    you: I know. Eight counts. Then you can go back to everyone else.
    > He finds himself in the mirror the way you'd find a stranger in a crowd. Politely. A little surprised.
justin: Five. Six. Seven. Eight.
@cam pushin
> Hyunjun's hand. Hyunjun's shoulders. And then it arrives in Hamin and, for once, he lets it.
> It goes through him clean. Wrist, elbow, the long line of his back, out along the other arm. Nothing in him is checking anything.
> In the mirror, he catches his own eye. He doesn't look away. It lasts perhaps two counts. It is the longest you've seen him be the subject of his own attention.
> Justin takes the wave off the end of his fingers and throws it at the ceiling.
hyunjun: There. That. {p}Tell me I'm not the only one who saw that.
justin[smile]: There he is.
hamin[smile]: It was one run.
justin: It was the run. I'm keeping it. That's the choreography now. You're in the middle forever.
@cam wide
> Hyunjun is already pulling his shoes on. The lace, you notice, has come undone.
hyunjun: Okay. Convenience store. One more stop before five. Who wants what?
hamin: Charlie wants the tuna triangle. Songha will say nothing, which means banana milk. Hanbi's fine, he ate, I watched.
hyunjun: And you?
hamin: Anything.
> Hyunjun doesn't move. He stands in the doorway with one shoe on, smiling, waiting like a very patient bus.
hyunjun: Hamin. I'm buying two of something whatever happens. You pick the something.
> The pause is long. Justin mimes checking a watch. Nobody rushes him.
hamin[thinking]: …The egg sandwich. The one with too much mustard.
hyunjun[smile]: Good. That's a preference.
justin: A *specific* one. I respect a man who knows his mustard.
hyunjun: Egg sandwich. Too much mustard. I'll remember that. I'd have asked years ago if I'd known there was an answer.
> He says it without any teasing in it at all. Hamin holds his eye, and nods, and doesn't take it back.
> They go, Justin still marking the wave with one hand as he walks, Hyunjun's voice already planning a second stop.
@hide justin
@hide hyunjun
@music quiet
@cam closeup hamin
> The speaker clicks off. The room is suddenly only a room: a mirror, some tape, the two of you in it.
> Hamin hasn't left the middle. He seems to have only just realised.
hamin: When we debut. {p}If. {p}Songha says "when". I'm practising "when".
hamin: There'll be a camera whose whole job is to find whoever's standing here.
hamin: I've always known where the back is. It's a good place. You can see who's tired from the back.
you: And in the middle?
hamin: In the middle people can see if you are.
> He scuffs the tape with the side of his shoe.
hamin[thinking]: The part I didn't expect is that I liked it. Just now. Those eight counts.
hamin: I want that. I want to be where the camera looks. I've never said it out loud. It sounds greedy in here.
* [sincere] "It doesn't sound greedy. It sounds like somebody finally counted himself."
    @rel hamin trust+1 romance+1
    hamin: …Nine cups.
    you: Nine cups. One of them in the middle.
    hamin[smile]: You keep using my own maths against me.
* [playful] "Greedy is Justin with Hyunjun's crackers. You asked for eight counts."
    @rel hamin closeness+1
    hamin[laugh]: Eight counts and an egg sandwich.
    you: A reign of terror.
    hamin: I'll try to live with myself.
* [quiet] Find his eyes in the mirror, on purpose, and don't be the first to look away. {if hamin.trust >= 3}
    @rel hamin romance+1 closeness+1
    > He notices. Of course he notices. He doesn't look at the floor. He looks back, steadily, as if he has decided to.
    > Two people in a mirror at a quarter past four, being looked at, and staying.
    hamin[smile]: …So that's what it's like from this side. {p}I could get used to it.
hamin: I'm scared of it. That's the plain word. I looked for a better one.
hamin: If I want it and it doesn't come, then I wanted it. There's no paper towel for that.
you: Noted. Hamin: scared. Wants it anyway.
hamin: You're keeping the list again.
you: Somebody has to. You keep leaving yourself off yours.
> He goes to the door and puts his hand on the light switch.
> Then he comes back, stands on the tape for one more second, entirely on his own, and nods at the person in the glass.
> Then he turns the lights off.
@goto hamin_ch3

=== hamin_ch3
@autosave
@bg studio_hall t=slow d=1.6
@music quiet
@time 04:22
@amb night
@cam wide
@show hamin neutral at=c
> The hallway outside the practice room is dim now. The stair lights have been going out one landing at a time, and the dark has reached the second floor.
> Hamin has his phone out. He isn't looking at it. He's listening to the building, the way you'd listen to someone breathing in the next room.
hamin: Can I do something quiet?
you: How quiet?
hamin: No talking. For a bit. I want to record it. The building. Before it isn't one.
hamin: You don't have to help. You can just walk with me.
> He presses record and holds the phone out at arm's length. Very seriously. You have seen this posture twice tonight. You know better than to breathe loudly near it.
> The hallway first. The hum behind eight dark doors, lower than it was at midnight, like a fridge in a house where everyone's asleep.
> Then the stairs. He goes down three steps, comes back up, goes down again. On the third stair from the top, the wood creaks.
> He holds the phone next to it and steps on it a second time. It creaks again, obligingly.
> He looks at you. Points at the stair. Raises his eyebrows: *did you know?*
> You shake your head. He looks quietly pleased to be the one who did.
> The lobby heater, which ticks as it cools. The kettle two floors up, which you can't hear, but he tilts the phone towards the ceiling anyway, to be fair to it.
> Then he turns to you, and tips the phone, and waits. Your turn. One sound.
* [quiet] Lead him to the front door, and let the phone hear the laundromat song through the glass.
    @rel hamin closeness+1 trust+1
    > It comes through thin and tinny, the same eight bars it has been playing since two thousand and ten.
    > He listens with his whole face. At the key change, the one that never quite lands, the corner of his mouth goes.
    > He holds up two fingers, then four, then seven. You have no idea what it means. Then you do. *Two forty-seven.* He's marking where the good part is.
* [playful] Walk him to the claw machine and press the button, so it plays its losing jingle.
    @rel hamin closeness+1
    @sfx claw
    > Five descending notes. The most-performed piece of music in the history of Studio 25.
    > He laughs without a sound, head down. The phone dips slightly. The recording will have a wobble in it, right there, forever.
    > He mouths a word at you. It is *Daniel*. You both nod, solemnly, in tribute.
* [curious] Mouth the question: which sound would *you* miss? Then follow where he goes.
    @rel hamin trust+1
    > He thinks. Then he walks back up to the third stair from the top and stands beside it like a man introducing a friend.
    > He steps on it going down. It creaks. He steps on it going up. It doesn't.
    > He shows you this twice. It only complains about people leaving. He seems to have thought about that a great deal.
> The last place he goes is the archive room.
@bg studio_archive t=fade d=1.4
@fx memory n=0.4
@cam wide
> Blue dark. Shelves of small glass boxes, and in the middle the core, turning slowly, dimmer than it was. It sounds like a held note.
> He records that too. A full minute of it. Neither of you moves.
@sfx echo
@textbox echo
echo: I note that I am being archived. It is an unusual sensation. I believe I approve.
echo: Owner Hamin. While you are recording. There is one item in RECORD 02 that I never resolved.
echo: At three minutes and fifty-eight seconds, a voice on the seawall calls a name. I have it filed as: static.
echo: I would prefer not to retire with static. If it is possible.
@textbox normal
> Hamin looks at the phone. Then at you. He breaks his own rule to say it, very low, so the recording has to lean in.
hamin: Would you say it? Into this one. {p}Your name.
hamin: You don't have to. It's yours. I'm only asking.
* [sincere] Say your name, plainly, the way he said it in the room.
    @rel hamin trust+1 romance+1
    you: {{playerName}}.
    > It sounds different in here. Like something being put on a shelf at the right height.
    > He doesn't move the phone away afterwards. He keeps it there a moment, as if names have a tail, like a bell does.
* [quiet] Lean in and say it almost under your breath, so the phone has to come closer.
    @rel hamin closeness+1 trust+1
    you: {{playerName}}.
    > He brings the phone nearer. Which brings him nearer. For the length of one word you are both very careful about breathing.
* [careful] "You say it. It's been in your recording for six years. It should be in your voice."
    @rel hamin trust+2
    > He considers it. He takes one slow breath. Then he holds the phone up to himself, which he has not done once tonight.
    hamin: {{playerName}}.
    > He says it the way he put the lanyard on the desk. A little apart from everything else. Like a guest.
@textbox echo
echo: Resolved. The word is {{playerName}}.
echo: It is shorter than I expected. For six years I imagined something with considerably more static in it.
echo: Thank you. I have nothing unresolved now. I did not know that was a condition an archive could be in.
@textbox normal
@cam closeup hamin
> The recording is still running. You take the phone out of his hand, gently, and turn it round, and hold it out to him at arm's length.
> He understands at once. You watch him want to talk about the weather.
you: One thing. Not the sea. You.
> He is quiet for a long time. The core turns. The counter on the screen climbs. You don't fill the gap, and you don't hand him anything to fill it with.
> This, you are learning, is the whole trick with him. You leave the gap, and you stay next to it.
hamin[thinking]: …I'm happy.
> He looks faintly startled, as if the word came out of someone standing behind him.
hamin: That's what this is. It took me a minute to find it. I don't use it much.
hamin: I've been fine for years. I'm very good at fine. This isn't that.
hamin[smile]: I'm happy. At four twenty-something in the morning, in a building that's about to be switched off. {p}For the record.
> You turn the phone back and press stop. The file sits at the top of his list, above the sea, with no name.
hamin: It needs a title.
* [playful] "'Building. Being Brave About It.'"
    @rel hamin closeness+1
    hamin[smile]: After the sock.
    you: In honour of the sock.
    > He types it in, full stops and all, and saves it with great ceremony.
* [sincere] "Name it after what you said. 'For the record.'"
    @rel hamin trust+1 romance+1
    hamin: That's just the last thing I said.
    you: It's the first thing you said about you without being asked twice.
    > He types it. His thumb hovers a moment over the word *record*, and then he lets it stand.
* [quiet] Give him the phone back without a word. Let him name it.
    @rel hamin trust+1
    > He thinks. He types slowly, deletes it, types again.
    > When he tilts the screen towards you it says: *04:29. Two people.*
    hamin: It's accurate.
    you: It's very accurate.
> In the blue dark, the core turns a little brighter for a second, the way a person does when they've been thanked.
@fx off
@goto hamin_ch4

=== hamin_ch4
@autosave
@bg studio_lounge t=fade d=1.2
@music quiet
@time 04:32
@amb room
@cam wide
@show hamin neutral at=c
> The third-floor lounge again. On the counter, where nine cups used to be, there is a convenience-store bag with a receipt stapled to it.
> On the receipt, in Hyunjun's handwriting: EGG. TOO MUCH MUSTARD. FOR HAMIN. NOT FOR JUSTIN. (JUSTIN I MEAN IT.)
> Hamin reads it. He puts it down without opening the bag, and picks up the kettle instead.
hamin: Hanbi will want something warm before we go. And Charlie never finished his.
you: Your sandwich is right there.
hamin: In a minute.
> He fills the kettle. He sets it on its base. He takes a cup from the stack, and then stands holding it, looking at the marker as if it's further away than it is.
> He writes HANBI on the cup. Then, underneath it, he writes HANBI again.
hamin: …That's the same person.
you: When did you get up this morning?
hamin: Six. We had practice at seven.
you: So you've been awake for twenty-two hours. On one cup of barley tea.
hamin: I'm fine.
> He reaches to switch the kettle on and presses the side of the toaster box instead. Nothing happens. He waits for it to boil anyway.
hamin: I'm fine. I'm just doing things slightly to the left.
* [sincere] "Sit down. I'll do the next ten minutes. All of it."
    @rel hamin trust+1 romance+1
    hamin: You don't know where things are.
    you: Bin's behind the counter. Heater needs hitting twice. Songha lies about eating. I've been paying attention.
    hamin[smile]: …You have.
* [playful] Take the marker and write on a fresh cup: HAMIN (SIT DOWN).
    @rel hamin closeness+1
    > You hold it up where he can read it. He reads it twice. Naturally.
    hamin[smile]: That's not how the brackets work. The brackets are for observations.
    you: I observe that you should sit down.
* [quiet] Take the kettle out of his hands and look at the sofa until he does too.
    @rel hamin trust+1
    > He holds on for half a second, out of habit. Then he lets go. His hands stay in the shape of a kettle for a moment, with nothing in them.
> He sits. He does it like someone who has read about sofas. Upright, at the edge, ready to be needed.
> You put the sandwich in his hands. He unwraps it with the caution of a person opening someone else's post.
> He takes a bite. He stops chewing. He looks at the sandwich with real respect.
hamin: There's too much mustard.
you: Is that a complaint?
hamin: No. It's correct. {p}It's exactly the right amount of too much.
> You make the tea. It is not difficult. From the sofa, you can feel him supervising with the back of your neck.
hamin: The lid sticks if you—
